    coverImagePosition: 50,
    body: `Willard Timothy Sutton, known around Greenville, North Carolina as "Tim," was a familiar and trusted face well before anyone thought to ask where his investment returns were actually coming from. He ran Greenville Auto World, LLC (GAW), a "buy here, pay here" used car dealership that sold vehicles to customers with weak credit and financed the loans itself — a real, ordinary small business. He was also the leader of the Tim Sutton Trio, a locally known gospel group, and that standing in Pitt County's church community became the recruiting engine for a second business almost nobody outside his inner circle understood: selling investors a stake in GAW's in-house auto loans.

Starting around 2019, Sutton told investors their money was being used to fund and service those buy-here-pay-here loan contracts, and that steady interest and principal payments from car buyers would keep their returns coming. According to federal prosecutors, that wasn't what was actually happening. Sutton sold the same underlying loan contracts to more than one investor at a time without telling either of them, forged customers' signatures on paperwork to manufacture loans that didn't exist, and handed over falsified vehicle title documents to back up the fiction — while using newly raised investor money to keep paying off earlier investors, the standard structure of a Ponzi scheme dressed up as a legitimate car lot's financing arm.

Investigators say more than $63 million moved through the scheme in total between 2019 and its collapse in 2023, much of it recycled repeatedly as it was paid out to earlier investors and then re-raised from new ones; more than 65 victims across Eastern North Carolina were left holding net losses of roughly $9 million once those churned payments were netted out. Sutton was charged with mail fraud, pleaded guilty on February 26, 2025, and was sentenced on July 2, 2025, to 63 months — five years and three months — in federal prison, followed by three years of supervised release, and ordered to pay $8,986,162 in restitution. "The defendant orchestrated a complex and brazen Ponzi scheme that exploited the trust of hard-working North Carolinians," Acting U.S. Attorney Daniel P. Bubar said at sentencing.

A buy-here-pay-here dealership is already, in a sense, an informal lender to customers other banks won't finance — which gave Sutton a built-in cover story that a stranger pitching a stock tip could never manufacture. Layered on top of a respected gospel singer's standing in his own church community, that cover meant investors weren't buying into an abstract fund they'd have to take on faith; they were "investing" in a business, and a man, they could see operating in their own town every day. That kind of proximity is exactly what made it easy to skip the paperwork checks — the county title records, the loan-contract originals — that would have shown the same car loan had already been sold to someone else.`,
