    coverImageCredit: 'Photo: Ebyabe, Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0) — the Sam M. Gibbons U.S. Courthouse in Tampa, Middle District of Florida, where Wexler and Bleignier were prosecuted',
    coverImagePosition: 50,
    body: `Paul Wexler, 56, of Spring, Texas, and Paul Bleignier, 64, of Seminole, Florida, ran a telemarketing company built around a single cold call: Medicare would cover an expensive cancer genetic screening — known in the industry as CGx testing — at no cost to the beneficiary. It was a real category of test, aimed at detecting inherited mutations linked to cancer risk, but the beneficiaries Wexler and Bleignier's callers targeted mostly hadn't asked for it, didn't need it, and in many cases never heard back about the results. What they were really generating, prosecutors said, was a stream of signed referrals that Wexler and Bleignier could bill to Medicare.

The referral pipeline ran on kickbacks. According to federal prosecutors, Wexler, Bleignier, and their co-conspirators solicited and received illegal kickback payments in exchange for steering Medicare beneficiaries toward CGx testing that was not eligible for Medicare reimbursement in the first place. Through the scheme, the pair caused Medicare to be billed $17.3 million, of which they personally were paid $5.2 million — money generated almost entirely from phone calls rather than any legitimate medical referral process.

Bleignier didn't stop there. While the genetic-testing criminal case against him was still pending, he opened a new clinical laboratory and enrolled it with Medicare — a process that requires disclosing anyone with a 5% or greater ownership stake in the business. Rather than disclose his own involvement, prosecutors said, Bleignier used other people's identities to obscure his ownership on the enrollment paperwork. That lab went on to bill Medicare for $3,012,156 in claims that were ineligible for reimbursement, of which it was paid $916,106 — a second fraud layered directly on top of the first, run while the first was already under prosecution.

Wexler pleaded guilty in April 2024 to conspiracy to commit health care fraud and wire fraud. Bleignier pleaded guilty twice: first in November 2022, to conspiracy to defraud the United States and to pay and receive illegal kickbacks, and again in November 2024, to making false statements in connection with health care matters over the concealed lab ownership. On November 13, 2025, Wexler was sentenced to four years in federal prison, and Bleignier was sentenced to two years for the genetic-testing kickback scheme plus a consecutive two-year term for falsifying the lab's ownership records. Each man was ordered to pay $5.2 million in restitution, and each forfeited $1.2 million, with Bleignier separately forfeiting the additional $916,106 tied to the lab scheme.

The case is a reminder that genetic-testing fraud against Medicare rarely needs a fake product to work — only a real one, aimed at a patient who never asked for it, wrapped in a phone pitch that sounds like a benefit rather than the setup for a bill the program will ultimately pay. And Bleignier's second scheme, opened while the first was already the subject of a federal prosecution, shows how easily the same ownership-disclosure loophole he'd already exploited once was still sitting there to be used again.`,
