    coverImage: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Phillip_Burton_Federal_Building_%26_United_States_Courthouse.jpg?width=1200',
    coverImageCredit: 'Marincyclist, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons',
    coverImagePosition: 50,
    body: `Note: The allegations below come from a Federal Trade Commission civil complaint filed in June 2026. Nobody named has been convicted of a crime, and as of this writing the case is an active, unresolved lawsuit — a court has issued a temporary restraining order and asset freeze while litigation continues, but the underlying claims have not been proven in court.

Genesis Tech was, by most outward measures, a legitimate success story: a Kyiv-founded technology company started in 2008 that grew into a roughly billion-dollar operation with more than 25 subsidiary products, over a billion cumulative app downloads, and partnerships with major platforms including Google and Facebook. The FTC's complaint, filed in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of California, alleges that underneath that legitimate footprint, founder-CEOs Vladimir Mnogoletny and Vasily Ulianov built a portfolio of consumer apps — including the fitness and diet apps MadMuscles, Harna, and Unimeal; the mental-health app Wisey, which the FTC says falsely implied it could diagnose and treat ADHD; the document tools PDF Guru and PDF Master; the fashion app Lumi; and the horoscope app Nebula — that shared a common playbook for turning a free trial into a recurring charge customers didn't expect and couldn't easily escape.

According to the FTC, the pattern across all of these products ran the same way: draw users in with a free or nearly-free offer, build engagement through a personalized quiz or assessment, obscure the auto-renewal terms in the checkout flow, and then charge full subscription prices on a recurring basis — with cancellation deliberately made difficult once a customer tried to get out. The agency says the operation generated roughly $250 million in revenue from just five of its products between early 2023 and mid-2025, and structured itself through an "ever-evolving web" of shell companies incorporated in Cyprus and Delaware, along with a habit of continually registering new corporate entities, opening new bank accounts, and launching new branded products, which the FTC alleges was done specifically to stay ahead of fraud monitoring and consumer complaints rather than for any ordinary business reason.

At the FTC's request, the court granted a temporary restraining order on June 17, 2026, freezing the assets of the fifteen corporate defendants and eight individual defendants named in the case and barring them from continuing the challenged billing practices while the case proceeds. Some defendants have since stipulated to preliminary injunctions; others have contested the FTC's case, and a preliminary injunction hearing was held in July 2026 to determine what restrictions continue while the underlying fraud claims are litigated. No trial date, settlement, or final judgment had been reached as of this writing.

The case is a reminder that a company doesn't need to be an obscure shell operation to end up accused of running a subscription trap at scale — Genesis Tech's products had real download numbers, real user bases, and real technology partnerships, which is exactly the kind of legitimate-looking surface regulators say let a hidden-renewal business model reach hundreds of millions of dollars in revenue before enough complaints piled up to draw federal scrutiny. As with any active federal case, the fair reading for now is that these are allegations the FTC believes it can prove — not a settled finding of fraud.`,
